WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:22.990
Cyrus the Great: The King of Kings
In the 6th century BC, Cyrus II of Persia

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:25.500
embarked on a mission of conquest.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.859
Kingdom after kingdom fell before him as he
formed the Achaemenid Empire, the largest

00:00:29.859 --> 00:00:33.050
empire that the world had ever seen up until
that point.

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:38.160
It kept growing under successors like Cambyses
and Darius until it, too, fell under the might

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.910
of Alexander the Great during the late 4th
century BC.

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:47.690
Cyrus left behind him a rich legacy, but one
which is still filled with uncertainties,

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:49.460
mythologies, and misapprehensions.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:55.510
He has a reputation not only as a conqueror,
but also as an innovative administrator and

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:57.280
a pioneer of human rights.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:03.670
How much of that is deserved we will try to
find out now as we look at the life of Cyrus,

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:06.350
the Great King.

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:14.720
Legendary Beginnings
Cyrus II was born ca. 600 BC in Anshan, an

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:18.180
ancient city ruled by his father, King Cambyses
I.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:22.860
His mother was Mandane, a daughter of Astyages,
king of the Median Empire.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:27.120
Greek historian Herodotus gives us a very
detailed and dramatic account of Cyrus’s

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.120
childhood.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:33.130
However, it leans heavily on the mythological
side but, then again, so do most ancient sources

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:34.930
that deal with Cyrus.

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:39.590
According to Herodotus, the marriage between
Cambyses and Mandane occurred after Astyages

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:45.010
had a prophetic dream where water flowed from
his daughter’s womb and flooded his kingdom.

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:50.020
He consulted with his magi who concluded that
his dream was a foreboding vision that meant

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.350
the downfall of his empire.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:56.410
Astyages wanted to place his daughter somewhere
safe and out of the way.

00:01:56.410 --> 00:02:00.520
That’s why he married her to Cambyses, a
Persian who ruled over a small and weak city

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:03.799
which would surely pose no threat to his kingdom.

00:02:03.799 --> 00:02:06.100
Soon afterwards, the Median king had another
dream.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:09.810
This time, vines sprouted out of his daughter’s
womb, covering the kingdom.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:14.200
Astyages and his magi took this to mean that
Mandane’s offspring was the one who would

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.200
prove to be his downfall.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:20.170
The conclusion was simple - the child had
to die.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:24.910
When Cyrus was born, Astyages gave him to
one of his most trusted servants, a Mede named

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:25.910
Harpagus.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:29.580
The king commanded him to take the child home,
kill him and bury him.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:30.580
Harpagus agreed, but couldn’t go through
with it.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:34.810
Instead, he gave the baby to a cowherd named
Mitradates and ordered him to do the evil

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:39.209
deed by leaving the child in the “most desolate
part of the mountains.”

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:42.050
Mitradates agreed, but also couldn’t bring
himself to do it.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:46.310
As it happened, his own wife had been pregnant
and had just given birth to a stillborn child.

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:50.610
They decided to pull the old switcheroo and
left their son in the mountains and raised

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:51.930
Cyrus as their own.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:55.880
The boy was not called Cyrus during this time
and, while Herodotus does not mention his

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.470
name, Strabo gives it as Agradates.

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:02.500
Cyrus lived as the son of a cowherd for ten
years until he was found out.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.500
One day, he was playing with the other children
in his village.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.290
He was proclaimed “king” and gave out
orders which one of the other boys refused

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:09.290
to obey.

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:11.520
To address this insult, the “king” had
the other boy beaten.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:16.270
His father, a noble Mede named Artembares,
complained to the real king.

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:20.430
Because Artembares was an important courtier,
Astyages himself went to talk with the son

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:23.610
of a cowherd and he soon realized his true
identity.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:27.960
Subsequently, he got the real story out of
Mitradates and, later, Harpagus.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.129
Cyrus got off easy.

00:03:29.129 --> 00:03:33.560
Upon renewed consultation with his magis,
Astyages concluded that, since Cyrus was made

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:37.640
“king” by the boys of his village, his
prophetic dream had been fulfilled and, therefore,

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:39.480
the child was no longer a threat.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:43.650
He was still of Persian blood, though, so
he was sent to live with his birth parents

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:44.709
in Anshan.

00:03:44.709 --> 00:03:49.580
The one who truly felt the wrath of Astyages
was Harpagus, the minion who disobeyed the

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:50.580
king’s order.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:55.769
Outwardly, Astyages showed no ill will towards
Harpagus and even invited him to a feast in

00:03:55.769 --> 00:03:57.700
honor of his newly-discovered grandson.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:02.450
There, he tricked his servant into eating
his own son who had been killed, dismembered,

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:04.750
boiled and roasted before the banquet.

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:10.140
Astyages revealed his malevolent act by presenting
Harpagus with his son’s head, hands, and

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:12.160
feet after he had finished eating.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:16.979
Publicly, Harpagus reacted calmly, simply
saying that whatever his king did pleased

00:04:16.979 --> 00:04:17.979
him.

00:04:17.979 --> 00:04:23.379
He collected his son’s remains, buried them
at home and began plotting his revenge.

00:04:23.379 --> 00:04:31.190
Conquering Media
In 559 BC, Cyrus ascended to the throne of

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:32.190
Anshan.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:36.310
Like his predecessor, he had to acknowledge
the authority of the Median Empire and pay

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:37.310
tribute.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:41.759
Unbeknownst to King Astyages, though, Harpagus
began courting the new king and encouraged

00:04:41.759 --> 00:04:46.409
him to get the Persians to rebel against Astyages,
promising that he will get the Medes to do

00:04:46.409 --> 00:04:47.430
the same.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:51.409
Cyrus was onboard with the plan, but needed
to find a way to unite the Persians.

00:04:51.409 --> 00:04:56.120
Back then, Persia consisted of multiple tribes
and he required all their strength to stand

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:57.339
against the Kingdom of Media.

00:04:57.339 --> 00:05:02.220
Cyrus’s own tribe was the Pasargadae, but
Herodotus also identified the Maraphii, the

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:09.180
Maspii, the Derusiaei, the Panthialaei, the
Germanii, the Dahae, the Mardi, the Dropici,

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:10.439
and the Sagartii.

00:05:10.439 --> 00:05:14.569
Cyrus summoned their representatives to him
and told each to bring a sickle.

00:05:14.569 --> 00:05:17.779
On the first day, he put them to work and
had them clear and prepare a stretch of land

00:05:17.779 --> 00:05:19.900
up to twenty furlongs long in each direction.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:24.479
On the second day, he had them wash and take
part in a sumptuous feast in a meadow where

00:05:24.479 --> 00:05:26.099
wine and food were plentiful.

00:05:26.099 --> 00:05:30.419
Afterwards, he simply asked them which day
they preferred and, obviously, everyone answered

00:05:30.419 --> 00:05:31.419
the second day.

00:05:31.419 --> 00:05:35.159
That is when Cyrus promised those who stood
by him only days like that free of toil and

00:05:35.159 --> 00:05:38.449
slavery, while those who opposed him would
have “labors unnumbered.”

00:05:38.449 --> 00:05:43.919
Most Persians resented having to submit to
the Medes in the first place, so Cyrus’s

00:05:43.919 --> 00:05:46.469
proposition was not exactly a hard sell.

00:05:46.469 --> 00:05:50.669
Even so, we do not know if all the tribes
joined in on the revolt, but there were enough

00:05:50.669 --> 00:05:53.490
for Cyrus to go to war against the Median
Empire.

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:58.750
In a moment of pure foolishness, Astyages
assigned Harpagus as commander of his army.

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:02.399
We don’t know if he forgot what he did to
his son or if he genuinely believed that his

00:06:02.399 --> 00:06:04.460
general had forgiven him for it.

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:09.779
Either way, Harpagus definitely remembered
and had waited patiently to enact his vengeance.

00:06:09.779 --> 00:06:14.509
He kept his end of the bargain with Cyrus
and convinced many Medes to betray Astyages.

00:06:14.509 --> 00:06:19.150
Therefore, as the Median army marched, many
soldiers deserted while others openly joined

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:20.279
the Persians.

00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:23.629
Those who remained loyal to the old king were
defeated in battle.

00:06:23.629 --> 00:06:28.339
Not left with much choice, Astyages assembled
another army from the men left in Ecbatana,

00:06:28.339 --> 00:06:31.649
the kingdom capital, and led it himself against
Cyrus.

00:06:31.649 --> 00:06:35.569
This new force mostly consisted of youths
and old men and they, too, rebelled against

00:06:35.569 --> 00:06:38.469
the king and surrendered Astyages over to
Cyrus.

00:06:38.469 --> 00:06:43.610
This happened in 550 BC, according to the
Nabonidus Chronicle, and Cyrus the Great entered

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:45.689
triumphantly into Ecbatana.

00:06:45.689 --> 00:06:50.680
The Persian king spared Astyages and kept
him around his court until his death.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:54.770
Cyrus also married one of the Median king’s
daughters, Amytis, which helped strengthen

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:58.159
his legitimacy as a ruler and pacify some
of the Median vassals.

00:06:58.159 --> 00:07:01.649
It was the first step in assembling the Achaemenid
Empire.

00:07:01.649 --> 00:07:09.300
The Lineage of Cyrus
You might be wondering why a Persian empire

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.789
founded by Cyrus was called the Achaemenid
Empire.

00:07:11.789 --> 00:07:16.690
It is worth taking a little time to explore
his lineage and try to present it as best

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:17.699
we understand it.

00:07:17.699 --> 00:07:22.830
The dynasty is named after Achaemenes, the
apical ancestor from which all the rulers

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:25.110
of the First Persian Empire descended.

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:28.729
Whether or not he actually existed, though,
is still a matter of some debate.

00:07:28.729 --> 00:07:32.490
The main source we have for his existence
is the Behistun Inscription, a giant rock

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:36.030
relief carved into Mount Behistun in the Zagros
Mountains.

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:40.349
Commissioned by King Darius the Great, the
inscription tells of his own rise to power.

00:07:40.349 --> 00:07:45.349
Scholars are fascinated by the Behistun Inscription
because it was vital in helping them understand

00:07:45.349 --> 00:07:49.969
cuneiform script as the inscription was written
in three different languages: Old Persian,

00:07:49.969 --> 00:07:51.289
Elamite, and Babylonian.

00:07:51.289 --> 00:07:55.629
In that regard, this monument is similar to
the Rosetta Stone which helped us decipher

00:07:55.629 --> 00:07:56.629
hieroglyphs.

00:07:56.629 --> 00:08:01.319
To us, it is relevant because, in the first
section, Darius traces his lineage all the

00:08:01.319 --> 00:08:03.330
way back to Achaemenes.

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:07.379
According to the family tree presented in
the inscription, Achaemenes founded the dynasty

00:08:07.379 --> 00:08:10.439
and was succeeded by his son, Teispes.

00:08:10.439 --> 00:08:14.710
There is some corroborating evidence not only
that Teispes existed, but that he captured

00:08:14.710 --> 00:08:16.899
the city of Anshan and became king.

00:08:16.899 --> 00:08:21.039
Here is where the family tree branches in
two directions as Teispes had two sons: Cyrus

00:08:21.039 --> 00:08:26.159
I, grandfather of Cyrus the Great, and Ariaramnes,
great-grandfather of Darius.

00:08:26.159 --> 00:08:31.099
When Darius became king, he married two of
Cyrus’s daughters, and later his granddaughter,

00:08:31.099 --> 00:08:33.180
thus merging the branches once again.

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:36.820
So it is possible that the lineage is that
straightforward, but here is the problem that

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:39.080
many scholars have with this notion.

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:42.440
Cyrus himself makes almost no mention of Achaemenes.

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:46.950
In the Cyrus Cylinder, which we will talk
about a bit later, Cyrus identifies himself

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:53.010
as a son of Cambyses, a grandson of Cyrus,
and a descendant of Teispes, but no Achaemenes.

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:59.010
The only reference is an inscription on a
column at the ruins of Palace P in Pasargadae

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:02.340
which says “I am Cyrus the king, an Achaemenid.”

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:05.430
This could have easily been added later and
some archaeologists believe that was, indeed,

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:06.430
the case.

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:10.410
They opine it was the work of Darius who wanted
to strengthen his claim to the throne and

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:15.430
establish continuity with the previous kings
who, until him, had always been direct descendants

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:17.780
of the previous rulers.

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:23.530
The War with Lydia
Back to Cyrus, he had conquered the Kingdom

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:27.430
of Media and brought it, as well as its vassals,
under his command.

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:29.830
Unsurprisingly, not everyone was ok with his
conquests.

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:35.170
Specifically, he earned the ire of King Croesus
of Lydia who was the brother-in-law of Astyages.

00:09:35.170 --> 00:09:38.990
Croesus was famed for his vast wealth and,
even today, you might hear people described

00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:40.670
as being “richer than Croesus”.

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:45.660
You might also know him for his ill-fated
counsel from the Oracle of Delphi.

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:51.770
He sent the priestesses there priceless gifts
such as statues and vessels made out of gold

00:09:51.770 --> 00:09:54.940
and asked them if he should send an army against
Cyrus.

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:59.520
The oracle, also known as the Pythia, replied
that “if Croesus goes to war he will destroy

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:00.520
a great empire.”

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:03.480
Never did it occur to him that it might be
his own.

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:07.430
So the Lydians crossed the Halys River into
Cappadocia and captured the city of Pteria,

00:10:07.430 --> 00:10:08.630
most likely in 547 BC.

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:11.430
Cyrus mustered his own army and marched to
regain his land.

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:15.690
The two sides met in battle and fought to
a stalemate with both sustaining heavy casualties.

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:17.930
Strategically, it was a victory for the Persians
as Croesus fell back to the Lydian capital

00:10:17.930 --> 00:10:18.930
of Sardis.

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:24.050
His plan was to wait out the winter and, in
spring, attack again with a larger army which

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:26.920
included his allies such as the Egyptians,
the Babylonians, and the Spartans.

00:10:26.920 --> 00:12:26.000
Foolishly, Croesus disbanded his remaining
soldiers, thinking that Cyrus would never

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:28.880
march on Sardis with winter fast approaching.

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:30.630
But the Persian king did just that.

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:34.680
Cyrus knew that his best shot at victory would
be a quick assault before Croesus gathered

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:35.680
reinforcements.

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:39.930
The two armies fought again in December at
the Battle of Thymbra where Cyrus scored a

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:41.690
decisive victory.

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:45.470
According to Herodotus, the winning strategy
came courtesy of Harpagus.

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:47.630
The Lydians had a large and powerful cavalry.

00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:51.960
To counter that, Harpagus advised Cyrus to
put his frontline soldiers on the dromedary

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:55.130
camels they brought along to carry food and
cargo.

00:12:55.130 --> 00:13:00.060
His reasoning was that horses were afraid
of camels and even hated their smell and would

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:01.590
turn to flee when they met.

00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:05.310
Indeed, this was the case, and many Lydian
soldiers were thrown off their mounts and

00:13:05.310 --> 00:13:09.400
quickly dispatched by the Persian infantry
running behind the camels.

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:10.980
Most of the Lydian army surrendered.

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:15.500
What was left of Croesus’s forces retreated
to Sardis but, of course, Cyrus followed closely

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:16.550
and laid siege.

00:13:16.550 --> 00:13:21.000
Croesus hoped that his allies might still
come and save the day if he could hold out

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:22.000
long enough.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:23.990
Alas, the siege lasted only 14 days.

00:13:23.990 --> 00:13:27.710
The Persians entered Sardis by scaling a section
of the wall that was completely unguarded.

00:13:27.710 --> 00:13:31.760
The grounds outside were so steep that they
were thought to be unclimbable, but a soldier

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:36.360
named Hyroeades proved that it was possible
and the rest of the Persians quickly followed

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:37.360
suit.

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:42.990
And so Sardis fell and, with it, the Kingdom
of Lydia which, after 650 years, became a

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:45.050
province of the Achaemenid Empire.

00:13:45.050 --> 00:13:48.450
The fate of Croesus is a question that remains
unanswered today.

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:52.860
According to Herodotus, Cyrus intended to
burn him on a pyre, but the god Apollo sent

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:54.460
a rain to put out the flames.

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:59.130
Seeing that the former Lydian king had divine
protection, Cyrus allowed him to stay on as

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:01.700
one of his courtiers, as he did with Astyages.

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:07.720
Of course, this is a rather fanciful tale
where the once-mighty king gets a happy ending.

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:11.420
The poet Bacchylides said that Croesus wanted
to burn himself alive, but that Apollo took

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:15.260
pity on him and sent him with the Hyperboreans,
a mythological race of giants, in the land

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:16.260
of the north.

00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:19.700
Scholars have interpreted this as a poetic
way of saying that the king had a peaceful

00:14:19.700 --> 00:14:20.700
death.

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:24.550
The last piece of evidence which suggests
that Croesus, indeed, died during the fall

00:14:24.550 --> 00:14:27.310
of Sardis, is the Nabonidus Chronicle, once
again.

00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:35.360
It states that, in the year 547/546 BC, during
the month of Ajaru (which corresponds to April/May),

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:39.410
Cyrus marched against a country, killed its
king and took its possessions.

00:14:39.410 --> 00:14:43.121
The only problem is that the inscription is
partially damaged and we can’t make out

00:14:43.121 --> 00:14:44.121
the name of the country.

00:14:44.121 --> 00:14:48.360
It could have been Lydia or it could have
been someplace else.

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:55.670
The Fall of Babylon
Cyrus continued his conquering ways and captured

00:14:55.670 --> 00:14:58.570
many cities in Asia Minor and Mesopotamia.

00:14:58.570 --> 00:15:04.210
So it happened until there was only one other
power left in all of western Asia - the Neo-Babylonian

00:15:04.210 --> 00:15:08.460
Empire, ruled by King Nabonidus whose chronicle
we have used and will continue to use as a

00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:09.680
main source.

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:12.650
Unsurprisingly, war erupted between the two
nations.

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:16.210
Unfortunately, while we know the major points,
we have very little detail on the war.

00:15:16.210 --> 00:15:22.370
The only large-scale battle that we know of
took place at Opis in 539 BC and it was a

00:15:22.370 --> 00:15:24.100
decisive victory for the Persians.

00:15:24.100 --> 00:15:28.540
Days later, they took the city of Sippar which
surrendered without any bloodshed.

00:15:28.540 --> 00:15:33.550
Interestingly enough, multiple sources indicate
that Cyrus walked into Babylon itself without

00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:34.550
battle.

00:15:34.550 --> 00:15:36.220
And here we arrive at another contentious
point.

00:15:36.220 --> 00:15:40.490
The Cyrus Cylinder, for instance, portrays
the Persian king as a liberator who was welcomed

00:15:40.490 --> 00:15:43.070
with open arms by the Babylonians.

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:47.110
This is not completely out of the question,
as Nabonidus might have been an unpopular

00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:53.040
king who angered his people by ignoring Marduk,
the patron god of Babylon, and trying to enact

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:55.952
a religious reform based around the worship
of the Moon god Sin.

00:15:55.952 --> 00:16:01.430
The Nabonidus Chronicle also says that the
army of Cyrus entered Babylon “without battle”,

00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:03.880
but does not provide us with any juicy details.

00:16:03.880 --> 00:16:07.440
This could mean that the people were happy
with their new king, but it could also fall

00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:12.290
in line with differing ancient accounts which
say that the two armies simply fought outside

00:16:12.290 --> 00:16:13.290
the capital.

00:16:13.290 --> 00:16:18.500
However it happened, the outcome was the same:
mighty Babylon had fallen and was now part

00:16:18.500 --> 00:16:24.560
of the Achaemenid Empire, never to be an independent
power again.

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:31.200
The Satrapies
Now Cyrus was King of the Four Corners of

00:16:31.200 --> 00:16:34.830
the World, a title meant to denote a person
who ruled over the whole of Mesopotamia.

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:39.960
However, he was faced with a new problem - how
would he oversee and manage the largest empire

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:41.120
the world had ever known?

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:44.820
Obviously, he couldn’t be everywhere at
once, but his kingdom would be full of issues

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:49.970
which demanded his attention - from serious
problems like riots, uprisings, and food shortages

00:16:49.970 --> 00:16:53.580
to mundane affairs like taxes and military
recruitment.

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:56.320
Cyrus employed a primitive version of a federal
government.

00:16:56.320 --> 00:17:01.000
He divided his empire into administrative
units known as satrapies which were run by

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:02.990
governors called satraps.

00:17:02.990 --> 00:17:07.169
Twenty-six satraps were appointed in Cyrus’s
time and they ruled in his name, dealing with

00:17:07.169 --> 00:17:09.600
all the issues that might arise in their regions.

00:17:09.600 --> 00:17:13.910
They had a lot of authority, but Cyrus had
the forethought not to give them too much

00:17:13.910 --> 00:17:14.949
unchecked power.

00:17:14.949 --> 00:17:20.020
There were still officials who were outside
the purview of satraps and reported straight

00:17:20.020 --> 00:17:21.020
to the king.

00:17:21.020 --> 00:17:25.350
They included army generals, finance officials,
as well as a special courtier who went from

00:17:25.350 --> 00:17:29.490
one satrapy to another to conduct inspections,
known as the “eye of the king”.

00:17:29.490 --> 00:17:33.249
Truth be told, this was a strategy that Cyrus
borrowed from the Median Empire.

00:17:33.249 --> 00:17:38.710
They used client kings who acted as governors
over smaller autonomous areas, but Cyrus was

00:17:38.710 --> 00:17:41.509
the first to employ them on a large scale.

00:17:41.509 --> 00:17:45.340
This innovative approach to the administration
of a large empire proved successful, even

00:17:45.340 --> 00:17:50.570
as Cyrus’s successors continued his expansionist
ways to add new kingdoms to their land.

00:17:50.570 --> 00:17:54.590
A testament to this was the fact that the
empire lasted for another 200 years after

00:17:54.590 --> 00:17:57.799
Cyrus’s death until it was conquered by
Alexander the Great.

00:17:57.799 --> 00:18:05.019
By comparison, Alexander’s empire immediately
collapsed and splintered after he was gone.

00:18:05.019 --> 00:18:11.100
The Cyrus Cylinder
Perhaps another factor which helped stabilize

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:14.490
the Achaemenid Empire was Cyrus’s alleged
policy of tolerance.

00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:19.140
There are multiple historical and religious
texts which assert that Cyrus allowed the

00:18:19.140 --> 00:18:22.720
people he conquered to practice their own
customs and religions.

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:27.679
He endeared himself to the Babylonians by
taking part in their New Year celebrations

00:18:27.679 --> 00:18:29.749
and obtaining the blessing of the god Marduk.

00:18:29.749 --> 00:18:33.570
In the Bible, it says that he allowed the
exiled Babylonian Jews to return to their

00:18:33.570 --> 00:18:34.570
lands.

00:18:34.570 --> 00:18:37.600
Consequently, he is referred to as an “anointed”
of God.

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:41.659
One artifact that was greatly responsible
for Cyrus’s reputation as a liberator and

00:18:41.659 --> 00:18:44.379
champion of human rights is the Cyrus Cylinder.

00:18:44.379 --> 00:18:48.179
Recovered from the ruins of Babylon, it now
sits in the British Museum and is often hailed

00:18:48.179 --> 00:18:51.299
as the first charter on human rights in the
world.

00:18:51.299 --> 00:18:55.299
This view was chiefly promoted in 1971 by
Mohammad Reza Pahlavi, the last Shah of Iran,

00:18:55.299 --> 00:18:57.121
during the 2,500-year celebration of the Persian
Empire.

00:18:57.121 --> 00:19:01.830
The idea was continued by the United Nations,
but it is one that historians and scholars

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:03.529
are not very happy with.

00:19:03.529 --> 00:19:08.429
They see the cylinder as a priceless artifact,
but one that is filled with standard propaganda.

00:19:08.429 --> 00:19:14.270
Basically, it portrays Nabonidus as an unjust
ruler and shows Cyrus as the liberator chosen

00:19:14.270 --> 00:19:16.669
by Marduk to come save the Babylonians.

00:19:16.669 --> 00:19:21.390
It is in line with Mesopotamian traditions
and should not be regarded as anything more

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:23.630
than a political declaration.

00:19:23.630 --> 00:19:32.420
The Demise of Cyrus
The death of Cyrus ends with one big question

00:19:32.420 --> 00:19:37.419
mark as there are, at least, five different
outcomes according to ancient sources.

00:19:37.419 --> 00:19:42.299
Although some assert that the king died peacefully
at home, most agree that Cyrus met his end

00:19:42.299 --> 00:19:43.299
in battle.

00:19:43.299 --> 00:19:46.730
Against whom, exactly, is the bone of contention.

00:19:46.730 --> 00:19:52.100
Herodotus, again, gives one of the most detailed
accounts, although even he admits that he

00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:57.009
heard multiple stories and this is simply
the one he deemed “most worthy of credit.”

00:19:57.009 --> 00:20:03.259
In 530 BC, Cyrus fought the Massagetae, a
nomadic Scythian tribe ruled by Queen Tomyris.

00:20:03.259 --> 00:20:07.049
Cyrus first extended an offer of marriage
to the queen which she refused, although another

00:20:07.049 --> 00:20:09.539
source presented Tomyris as the wife of Cyrus.

00:20:09.539 --> 00:20:13.499
Either way, the two armies were supposed to
meet in open battle, but Cyrus set a trap

00:20:13.499 --> 00:20:16.271
for the Massagetian troops and killed many
of them, including the general Spargapises

00:20:16.271 --> 00:20:18.059
who also happened to be the queen’s son.

00:20:18.059 --> 00:20:21.909
Angered by this deception, Tomyris gathered
all her remaining forces and met the Persians

00:20:21.909 --> 00:20:24.749
in a fierce battle where she emerged triumphant.

00:20:24.749 --> 00:20:29.600
Most of the Persians had been slain, Cyrus
included, ending his reign of 29 years.

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:33.529
Not satisfied, Tomyris tracked down his body,
decapitated him and dipped Cyrus’s head

00:20:33.529 --> 00:20:37.980
in a pool of blood so that the king might
fill his bloodlust which cost Tomyris the

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:45.620
death of her son.

00:20:45.620 --> 00:20:47.779
The Tomb of Cyrus
The remains of Cyrus were interred in a mausoleum

00:20:47.779 --> 00:20:51.110
in the capital of Pasargadae, named after
his tribe.

00:20:51.110 --> 00:20:56.110
The ruins of this ancient city still exist
today near the city of Shiraz, in modern day

00:20:56.110 --> 00:20:59.640
Iran, and the most treasured landmark is the
tomb of Cyrus.

00:20:59.640 --> 00:21:03.570
Oddly enough, the tomb might have not been
around today were it not for the greatest

00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:08.140
enemy of the Achaemenid Empire - Alexander
the Great, who was an admirer of Cyrus.

00:21:08.140 --> 00:21:12.320
Greek historian Arrian related how the Macedonian
king paid a visit to Cyrus’s tomb after

00:21:12.320 --> 00:21:13.669
conquering his empire.

00:21:13.669 --> 00:21:18.350
He was deeply angered to discover that thieves
had desecrated it and stolen almost everything

00:21:18.350 --> 00:21:19.350
of value.

00:21:19.350 --> 00:21:23.249
The golden lid on top of Cyrus’s coffin
was gone; his body had been thrown to the

00:21:23.249 --> 00:21:27.590
floor, perhaps in a failed attempt to steal
the entire coffin which proved too heavy.

00:21:27.590 --> 00:21:32.009
Alexander ordered the tomb repaired and Cyrus’s
remains restored to their proper place.

00:21:32.009 --> 00:21:35.590
He also had the guards of the monument arrested
and tortured so they would give him the names

00:21:35.590 --> 00:21:36.750
of the thieves.

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:41.179
Also there was an inscription which does not
exist anymore so the exact wording is iffy.

00:21:41.179 --> 00:21:47.220
It said: “O man, I am Cyrus, son of Cambyses,
who founded the empire of Persia

00:21:47.220 --> 00:21:48.799
and ruled over Asia.

00:21:48.799 --> 00:22:15.309
Do not grudge me my monument.”

